        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 776,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Marks the elements that are nan (\"not a number\"), and counts them.\n\nnan is what a calculation gives when it has no answer: 0 divided by 0, the mean of nothing, the square root of a negative in some libraries, a sensor that dropped out and was filled with a placeholder. It spreads - anything added to nan is nan - so a single one can quietly turn a sum, a mean or a whole filter's output into nan. np.isnan finds them so you can mask them, replace them, or just notice them.\n\nInfinity (inf, -inf) is NOT nan: it is a number, only too large. np.isfinite catches both kinds.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.isnan\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one; a message can say nan and inf). Every input is tested and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmask:\nTrue where the element is nan, the same shape as the input.\n\ncount:\nHow many elements are nan. Sent before the mask.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 nan 3 inf -inf 0: only the second value is nan, so the mask is false true false false false false and the count is 1. A metro re-sends the message five times a second; type nan somewhere else and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.isfinite marks every value that is an ordinary number - neither nan nor infinite. np.nan_to_num replaces nan and infinity with numbers you choose. np.nanmean, np.nanmedian and np.nansum skip the nans instead.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Marks the elements that are nan (\"not a number\"), and counts them.\n\nnan is what a calculation gives when it has no answer: 0 divided by 0, the mean of nothing, the square root of a negative in some libraries, a sensor that dropped out and was filled with a placeholder. It spreads - anything added to nan is nan - so a single one can quietly turn a sum, a mean or a whole filter's output into nan. np.isnan finds them so you can mask them, replace them, or just notice them.\n\nInfinity (inf, -inf) is NOT nan: it is a number, only too large. np.isfinite catches both kinds.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.isnan\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one; a message can say nan and inf). Every input is tested and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmask:\nTrue where the element is nan, the same shape as the input.\n\ncount:\nHow many elements are nan. Sent before the mask.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 nan 3 inf -inf 0: only the second value is nan, so the mask is false true false false false false and the count is 1. A metro re-sends the message five times a second; type nan somewhere else and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.isfinite marks every value that is an ordinary number - neither nan nor infinite. np.nan_to_num replaces nan and infinity with numbers you choose. np.nanmean, np.nanmedian and np.nansum skip the nans instead."
        },
